267 0 obj<</A 1013 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid displays vectors drawn from the origin. One vector labelled v equals 1, 2 or 1 plus 2 i points up and to the right. Another vector labelled w equals negative 1, 1 or negative 1 plus i points up and to the left. A vertical vector labelled w plus v equals 0, 3 or 3 i extends straight upward. A shaded quadrilateral connects the tips of these vectors.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 767 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
268 0 obj<</A 1014 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid shows a sector formed from the origin. A straight line extends from the origin to an outer point, creating an angle labelled t radians along the horizontal axis. A curved boundary connects the outer point back to the horizontal axis, with an arrow along the curve labelled arc length t. The shaded region fills the area between the straight line, the curve, and the horizontal axis.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 800 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
269 0 obj<</A 1015 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid shows three shaded vertical rectangles representing a periodic function. The horizontal axis is marked at negative pi, pi, and 2 pi. Each rectangle extends from the horizontal axis up to 1 on the vertical axis. One rectangle spans from negative pi to 0, the next from 0 to pi, and the third from pi to 2 pi, repeating the same height and shape.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 807 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
270 0 obj<</A 1016 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid shows a shaded rectangular region from x equals 0 to x equals pi with height 1. The horizontal axis is marked at negative pi, negative pi over 2, pi over 2, and pi. A wavy curve oscillates near y equals 0 on the left and near y equals 1 across the shaded region. A dashed horizontal line appears slightly above y equals 1.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 808 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
271 0 obj<</A 1017 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid displays three curves plotted against t. One smooth curve increases gradually from near 0 to near 1. A second curve rises more steeply, passing through the midpoint earlier. A third curve extends from about negative 1, crosses 0 near the vertical axis, and approaches 1. All curves level off near a value of 1 for large positive t.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 810 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
272 0 obj<</A 1018 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid shows a curve with very large values near x equals 0, dropping rapidly to near 0 by x equals 1. The curve stays low between x equals 1 and 4, then rises sharply after x equals 4, exceeding 24 near x equals 5 and approaching 120 by x equals 6. Dashed horizontal reference lines mark values at 6, 24, and 120.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 813 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
273 0 obj<</A 1019 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid shows two functions plotted against t. A curved graph labelled t cubed minus 1 decreases slightly for negative t, crosses the horizontal axis near t equals 1, and then rises steeply. A straight dashed line labelled 3 t minus 3 increases at a constant rate and also crosses the horizontal axis at t equals 1. Both graphs intersect at that point.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 818 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
274 0 obj<</A 1020 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid shows a smooth, symmetric bell-shaped curve centred on the vertical axis. The curve reaches a maximum value of 1 at x equals 0, then decreases evenly on both sides. As x moves toward negative 3 and positive 3, the curve approaches 0. The horizontal axis is marked from negative 3 to 3, and the vertical axis from 0 to 1.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 820 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
275 0 obj<</A 1021 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid shows two curves plotted against the horizontal axis. A solid curve increases smoothly from near 0 on the left and levels off close to 1 on the right, passing about 0.5 near the vertical axis. A dashed curve forms a low hump, rising to a peak near 0.25 around x equals 0, then decreasing toward 0 on both sides.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 822 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
276 0 obj<</A 1022 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid shows two vectors drawn from the origin and one plotted point. One vector labelled v equals 1, 2 points upward to the right. Another vector labelled u equals negative 1 over square root of 2, 1 over square root of 2 points upward to the left. A point labelled x equals 1, one half is plotted to the right of the vertical axis.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 825 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
277 0 obj<</A 1023 0 R/Alt(Two related plots are shown. On the left, a three dimensional surface rises and falls smoothly over a square base with x and y values from negative 1 to 1. On the right, a curve oscillates across the horizontal axis between about negative 0.2 and 0.2, with decreasing ripples near the origin and larger waves toward the edges.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 830 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
278 0 obj<</A 1024 0 R/Alt(A vector triangle shows a horizontal vector u and a slanted vector v forming an angle theta. The slanted side is labelled norm of v. Its horizontal projection is labelled norm of v cosine theta. A vertical dashed line shows norm of v sine theta with a right angle at the base. The remaining base is labelled norm of u minus norm of v cosine theta, and the right side is labelled norm of v minus u.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 832 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
279 0 obj<</A 1025 0 R/Alt(Two side by side contour plots show elliptical level curves centred near the origin, with axes ranging from negative 2 to 2. In each plot, a horizontal arrow points leftward from a point near x equals 1 and y equals 1. In the left plot, the arrow ends at a dashed vertical line, while in the right plot the arrow extends smoothly without a dashed boundary.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 834 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
280 0 obj<</A 1026 0 R/Alt(Two dot plots show F of p k versus k for k from 1 to 6. In the left plot, the points decrease steadily from about 1 at k equals 1 to near 0.1 at k equals 6. In the right plot, the value starts near 1 at k equals 1, drops to around 0.55 near k equals 3 and 4, then rises again to about 0.7 at k equals 6.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 835 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
281 0 obj<</A 1027 0 R/Alt(Two related plots are shown. On the left, a smooth S shaped curve increases from near 0 at x equals negative 4 to near 1 at x equals 4, passing about 0.5 near x equals 0. On the right, a three dimensional surface rises smoothly from low values on the left to higher values on the right, forming a sloped ridge over x and y values from negative 4 to 4.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 840 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
282 0 obj<</A 1028 0 R/Alt(Two square plots are shown side by side with axes from negative 3 to 3. The left plot displays a smooth, continuous pattern of light and dark curved bands forming a symmetric, star like shape centred at the origin. The right plot shows a related pattern reduced to bold curves and regions, highlighting the main contour lines and symmetry around the centre.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 841 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
283 0 obj<</A 1029 0 R/Alt(A simple neural network diagram shows three input nodes labelled x 1, x 2, and x 3. Each input connects by arrows with weights w 1, w 2, and w 3 to a central unit labelled u. The signal then passes through an arrow labelled s to a final box labelled Output, representing the network result.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 847 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
284 0 obj<</A 1030 0 R/Alt(A three dimensional surface is shown over a rectangular base with x and y axes ranging from about negative 2 to 2. The surface varies smoothly in height between roughly negative 1 and 1, forming gentle hills and shallow valleys. A central dip appears near the middle, with higher regions toward opposite corners. The surface is enclosed within a transparent three dimensional frame.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 849 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
285 0 obj<</A 1031 0 R/Alt(A vector diagram shows a horizontal vector x and a slanted vector y forming an angle theta at the left. The slanted vector is labelled norm of y. Its horizontal projection along the base is labelled norm of y cosine theta. A vertical dashed line drops to the base with a right angle marker, ending at a point labelled z above the base near the tip of x.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 871 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
286 0 obj<</A 1032 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid shows a shaded sector extending from the origin. One boundary is a straight vector labelled z, and the other boundary is a curved arc ending at a vector labelled e to the power i t z. An arrow inside the shaded region marks the angle t radians between the two boundaries. The shaded area fills the region swept as the vector rotates through the angle t.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 898 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
287 0 obj<</A 1033 0 R/Alt(A three dimensional box shows a shaded plane labelled W spanned by two vectors marked column 1 and column 2. A vector b rises upward from the plane. A dotted perpendicular drops from b to the plane, ending at a point labelled b tilde. The diagram illustrates projecting vector b onto the subspace W inside the three dimensional space.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 904 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
288 0 obj<</A 1034 0 R/Alt(Three small grids of x marks are shown side by side. In each grid, a square outline highlights a different group of four x marks. In the left grid, the highlighted square is at the top left. In the middle grid, the highlighted square is centred. In the right grid, the highlighted square is at the top right. The remaining x marks appear unhighlighted in each grid.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 906 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
289 0 obj<</A 1035 0 R/Alt(An irregular polygon is shown on a coordinate plane with a vertical axis at the left and a horizontal axis at the bottom. A tall, narrow polygon with a grid pattern stands upright near the vertical axis. Below it, a smaller slanted polygon with a dotted interior appears. To the right, a large triangular region slopes upward from left to right, filling the lower corner of the plane.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 916 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
290 0 obj<</A 1036 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid shows a straight line labelled L passing through the origin and slanting upward from bottom left to top right. The horizontal axis runs from about negative 3 to 3, and the vertical axis from about negative 6 to 6. A point on the vertical axis above the origin is marked and labelled A of L, indicating the lines associated value on that axis.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 922 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
291 0 obj<</A 1037 0 R/Alt(A Venn diagram shows two overlapping circles. The left circle is labelled A and the right circle is labelled B. The overlapping central region where the two circles intersect is labelled A intersection B. \n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 937 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
292 0 obj<</A 1038 0 R/Alt(Two plots are shown side by side on coordinate grids. The left plot shows a shaded rectangle from x equals 0 to x equals 1 with height 1, representing a step region. The right plot shows a piecewise linear graph that rises from 0 at x equals 0 to 1 at x equals 1, then remains flat at 1 until x equals 2.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 944 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
293 0 obj<</A 1039 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid shows a piecewise step function. From x equals 0 to x equals 1, three horizontal segments appear at increasing heights, with solid and open endpoints marking changes. At x equals 1, the function jumps to a higher constant level and remains flat from x equals 1 to x equals 2. The shaded regions emphasise the different constant values across each interval.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 945 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
294 0 obj<</A 1040 0 R/Alt(Two plots are shown side by side. The left plot displays multiple discrete distributions using dots and vertical stems across values from about 1 to 30, with peaks at different positions. The right plot shows corresponding cumulative step curves over the same range, each increasing from 0 toward 1 at different rates. The figures compare how probability mass and cumulative values shift across the horizontal axis.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 947 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
295 0 obj<</A 1041 0 R/Alt(Two bar charts are shown side by side with categories labelled 1 through 6 on the horizontal axes. In the left chart, bar heights vary, increasing from category 1 to a peak near category 5, then decreasing at category 6. In the right chart, all bars have nearly equal heights, indicating a uniform distribution across the same six categories.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 955 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
296 0 obj<</A 1042 0 R/Alt(A directed graph shows four nodes labelled 0, 1, 2, and 3 connected by arrows. Node 2 has a self loop. Arrows link node 2 with node 0, node 0 with node 1, and node 3 with nodes 0 and 1, with arrows in both directions between nodes 0 and 3. To the right, a four by four matrix of zeros and ones represents the adjacency relationships.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 965 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
297 0 obj<</A 1043 0 R/Alt(An undirected graph shows four nodes labelled 0, 1, 2, and 3. Node 2 has a self loop. Edges connect node 2 to nodes 0 and 3, node 0 to nodes 1 and 3, and node 3 to node 1, forming a diamond shape. To the right, a four by four matrix of zeros and ones represents the adjacency relationships between the nodes.\n␀)/K 1/P 35 0 R/Pg 965 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
298 0 obj<</A 1044 0 R/Alt(A directed graph shows four nodes labelled 0, 1, 2, and 3 arranged in a square. Each node has a self loop. Arrows connect every pair of distinct nodes, with arrows shown in both directions between all node pairs. The diagram represents a fully connected directed network with self loops at each node.\n␀)/K 2/P 35 0 R/Pg 965 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
299 0 obj<</A 1045 0 R/Alt(A directed graph shows two nodes labelled alpha and beta connected by a single arrow from alpha to beta. Both nodes have multiple self loops, with arrows starting and ending at the same node. The loops around beta are more numerous than those around alpha.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 966 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
300 0 obj<</A 1046 0 R/Alt(A neural network diagram shows two input nodes labelled v 1 and v 2 feeding into three intermediate nodes labelled 3, 4, and 5. Each input connects to all three intermediate nodes by directed arrows. The intermediate nodes then connect by arrows to a single Output node on the right, indicating a feedforward network structure.\n␀)/K 1/P 35 0 R/Pg 966 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
301 0 obj<</A 1047 0 R/Alt(A tree diagram shows a Data Set at the top branching into two groups labelled A and B. Group A splits into a and b, with a further dividing into alpha and beta. Group B splits into c and d. Arrows indicate hierarchical relationships from the data set to each subgroup, illustrating a step by step classification structure.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 967 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
302 0 obj<</A 1048 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid shows a smooth curve plotted from x equals 0 to x equals 1. The curve starts near y equals 0, rises quickly to a single peak slightly above 0.3 around x equals 0.3, then gradually decreases back toward y equals 0 as x approaches 1. The shape forms a single hump with no sharp corners or breaks.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 969 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
303 0 obj<</A 1049 0 R/Alt(Two contour plots are shown side by side. The left plot displays many closely spaced contour lines forming several peaks and depressions, with a vertical scale labelled from about 65 to 416. The right plot shows smoother, broader contour regions with fewer internal variations, using a scale from about 94 to 329.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 973 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
304 0 obj<</A 1050 0 R/Alt(Two large pixelated numerals are shown on a plain background. On the left is the number 3, formed from small square blocks arranged in a curved vertical shape. On the right is the number 7, formed from square blocks creating a flat top bar with a diagonal downward stroke.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 974 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
305 0 obj<</A 1051 0 R/Alt(Two large pixelated numerals are shown on a plain background. On the left is the number 2, formed from small square blocks arranged in a stepped, curved shape. On the right is the number 3, formed from thicker horizontal and vertical block segments creating a squared, digital style.\n␀)/K 1/P 35 0 R/Pg 974 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
306 0 obj<</A 1052 0 R/Alt(A large block based numeral 3 is formed from square tiles in varying shades. The shape is tall and vertical, with three horizontal segments connected by staggered blocks.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 975 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
307 0 obj<</A 1053 0 R/Alt(A rectangular grid displays rows labelled x, sigma of x, sigma squared of x, sigma cubed of x, and sigma to the fourth of x. Each row contains square cells shaded in three tones representing values 0, 1, and 2.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 977 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
308 0 obj<</A 1054 0 R/Alt(A rectangular grid shows rows labelled x, F of x, F squared of x, F cubed of x, and F to the fourth of x. Each row contains square cells shaded in different tones to indicate changing values across iterations. The shading becomes more uniform toward the lower rows, showing convergence under repeated application of F. Below the grid, small icon patterns illustrate corresponding block arrangements for selected steps.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 978 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
309 0 obj<</A 1055 0 R/Alt(Two small directed graphs are shown side by side, each with nodes labelled 0 and 1. In both graphs, arrows run in both directions between the two nodes. Each node also has self loops. The arrangement of the loops differs between the two graphs, illustrating alternative directed transition structures between the same pair of states.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 979 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
310 0 obj<</A 1056 0 R/Alt(A directed graph shows four nodes labelled U 1, U 2, U 3, and U 4 arranged in a diamond shape. Arrows connect U 3 and U 1 in both directions, and U 3 and U 4 in both directions. U 4 and U 2 are also connected by arrows in both directions. A single arrow points upward from U 4 to U 1, and another arrow points from U 1 to U 2.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 984 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
311 0 obj<</A 1057 0 R/Alt(Four graph examples are shown. The top left is a directed triangle with nodes 1, 2, and 3, including a self loop at node 2. The top right is an undirected triangle connecting nodes 1, 2, and 3. The bottom left is a directed tree with root a pointing to b, c, and d. The bottom right is a branching tree structure with multiple levels and leaf nodes.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 987 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
312 0 obj<</A 1058 0 R/Alt(A feedforward neural network diagram shows two input nodes v 1 and v 2 in layer 1 connected by arrows to three hidden nodes a, b, and c in layer 2. Each hidden node receives inputs from both inputs. The hidden nodes then connect by arrows to two output nodes o 1 and o 2 in layer 3. Arrows indicate the direction of information flow through the network.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 997 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
313 0 obj<</A 1059 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid shows a straight dashed line starting at the origin and rising diagonally upward to the right. The horizontal axis runs from about negative 2 to 2, and the vertical axis from 0 to about 2. The line increases at a constant rate, passing through points near 1, 1 and 2, 2, indicating a linear relationship.\n␀)/K 0/P 35 0 R/Pg 1000 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
314 0 obj<</A 1060 0 R/Alt(A coordinate grid shows many scattered points plotted across the horizontal axis from 0 to about 1000. The points cluster closely around a horizontal line near y equals 3.5, with wider variation on the left that gradually narrows toward the right. A solid horizontal reference line runs through the centre of the point cloud, indicating a stable value approached as the horizontal values increase.\n␀)/K 1/P 35 0 R/Pg 955 0 R/S/Figure/T()>>endobj
